        "title": "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "c3eafdd8-07a8-5eeb-996a-7c5b2fca35e0",
        ":question/problem": "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{53}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\frac{13}{4}}=1?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=53$ and ${b}^{2}=\\frac{13}{4}.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=53,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{53}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=\\frac{13}{4},$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{\\frac{13}{4}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=\\sqrt{53},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{53}=2\\sqrt{53},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}=\\sqrt{13}.$",
